= Introduction

== Intuitions in Differential Geometry

Recall in differential geometry, we can define the cotangent space at a point $p$ of a smooth manifold $M$ as the dual of the tangent space at $p$.

A typical object in the cotangent space, called a _cotangent vector_. Any smooth function $f in C^oo (M)$ gives a cotangent vector
$ d f_p in T^*_p M, quad d f_p (v) = v(f) $

We can associate the cotangent space together to get the cotangent bundle
$ T^* M := union.big_(p in M) T^*_p M $
Which is a smooth manifold projection $p:T^*M -> M$ with each fiber $T^*_p M$ being a vector space. The smooth sections of the cotangent bundle are called _differential 1-forms_ on $M$, denoted as $Omega^1(M)$ and forms a module over $C^oo (M)$.

#remark[
  We somehow find out that for smooth manifolds, we use a module instead of a sheaf of modules. This reveals that manifolds are actually somehow affine-geometric objects. Actually Whitney's embedding theorem tells us, that any smooth manifold can be embedded into some Euclidean space, which means it is in form of
  $ Spec^(C^oo) RR{x_1,...,x_n} / (f_1,...,f_m) $
  here $RR{x_1,...,x_n}$ is the $C^oo$-algebra of smooth functions on $RR^n$ and $(f_1,...,f_m)$ is the ideal generated by $f_1,...,f_m$. 
]

== Local Rings

#definition(title:[Local Ring])[
  A _local ring_ is a commutative ring $R$ associated with a unique maximal ideal $frak(m)$.
]

We call it a local ring, because geometrically it is basically the local information of a point on a scheme.

#claim[
  If $R$ is a local ring, then $R^times = R without frak(m)$.
]

#proof[
  Leave as an exercise to the reader 😈.
]

#example[
  For a manifold $M$ and a point $p in M$, the stalk of the structure sheaf $cal(O)_(M,p)$ (which is, all functions smooth at point $p$, i.e. all smooth functions defined on some open neighborhood of $p$) is a local ring with the unique maximal ideal
  $ frak(m)_(M,p) = { f in cal(O)_(M,p) | f(p) = 0 } $
]

Let's get back in algebraic geometry.

#construction(title:[Local ring of a point of a scheme])[
  For $X = Spec A$, and $frak(p) in Spec A$, the stalk of the structure sheaf $cal(O)_(X,frak(p))$ is basically the localization
  $ cal(O)_(X,frak(p)) = A_(frak(p)) = { a/s | a in A, s in A without frak(p) } $
  For schemes this construction compates with the above definition, by basically descending to the affine case.
]

#remark[
  How you should picture localizations is that, if you have an ideal $(f) ideal A$, where you view $V(f) = Spec A/(f)$ is a closed subscheme of $Spec A$, then the localization $A_((f))$ is basically the open subscheme $D(f) := Spec A without V(I)$. Thus the localization of a ideal, or prime ideal is the limit of open neighborhoods
  $ A_(frak(p)) = varprojlim(f in.not frak(p)) A_((f)) $
  taking $Spec$ gives
  $ Spec A_(frak(p)) = varprojlim(f in.not frak(p)) D(f) $
  This also gives sense to the following proposition:
]

#proposition[
  For all $x in X$ (scheme), the stalk $cal(O)_(X,x)$ is a local ring with the unique maximal ideal
  $ frak(m)_(X,x) = { f in cal(O)_(X,x) | f(x) = 0 } $
]

The maximal ideal $frak(m)$ of a local ring $R$ identifies all "infinitesimal" information of the ring, while the quotient
$ kappa(frak(m)) := R/frak(m) $
is called the _residue field_ of $R$, which is the "value field" of fucntions defined at that point.

#example[In the manifold case, the residue field is always $RR$.]

#example[
  Consider $Spec ZZ$, where you view every integer a function on a curve consists of each prime $p$, then for point $p in Spec ZZ$, the stalk is the local ring $ZZ_((p))$, where the maximal ideal is $frak(m) = (p)ZZ_((p))$ and the residue field is $FF_p$. A function $a in ZZ$ is evaluated at $p$ as $a(p) = a mod p in FF_p$.
]


= Zariski Cotangent Space

== The Taylor Expansion

Once you get why the maximal ideal $frak(m)$ of a local ring $R$ identifies all "infinitesimal" information of the ring, you can think about the Taylor expansion of a function $f$ at a point. Firstly, we can consider the _$frak(m)$-adic filtration_ of $R$
$ R supset frak(m) supset frak(m)^2 supset frak(m)^3 supset ... $
Since $frak(m)$ is all the functions vanishing at the point, $frak(m)^2$ is all the functions vanishing at the point with their first derivatives also vanishing... and so on (you can try to make this precise) where elements in $frak(m)^2$ must can be written as a sum of products of functions vanishing at the point, which means they vanish at the point at least two times. 

#remark[
  We can even define
  $ ord_(frak(m)) (f) : R -> NN union {oo}, quad f |-> sup{n >= 0 : f in frak(m)^n}, quad 0|->oo $
  We can compare it to holomorphic functions, on a complex manifold $M$ (specially an open subset of $CC$), the local ring at a point $a$ is the ring of germs of holomorphic functions at $a$ (holomorphic at $a$), and the maximal ideal $frak(m)$ is $(z-a)$, hence $frak(m)^n = ((z-a)^n)$, thus this valuation actually measures the order of vanishing of a holomorphic function at a point.
]

If we consider every function $f in R$ under $R/frak(m)^n$, we can think of it as the Taylor expansion of $f$ at the point. We can even define them formally as limit
$ R -> varprojlim(n) R/frak(m)^n $
knowned as the $frak(m)$-adic completion, but that's not the topic of this lecture note. Formal and adic geometry is a important subtopic in algebraic geometry.

== Definition of Zariski Cotangent Space

#definition(title:[Zariski Cotangent Space])[
  For a scheme $X$ and a point $x in X$, the _Zariski cotangent space_ at $x$ is defined as
  $ T^*_(X,x) := frak(m)_(X,x) / frak(m)_(X,x)^2 $
  as a $kappa(x)$ vector space, makes sence because $frak(m) dot (frak(m)/frak(m)^2) simeq 0$.
]

Now, if we consider
$ R/frak(m)^2 simeq k plus.o (frak(m)/frak(m)^2) $
which is exactly the first order Taylor expansion of a function $f in R$, where you can think of
$ f = underbrace(f(x),"order-0") + underbrace(d_x f,"order-1") quad (mod frak(m)^2) $
here we define $d_x f = [f - f(x)] in T^*_(X,x)$, this satisfies the _Leibniz rule_
$ d_x (f g) = f(x) d_x g + g(x) d_x f $
The proof is again left as an exercise to the reader 😈.

#example[
  For $X = AA^2_k$ and $x = (0,0)$. Then
  $ R = k[u,v]_((u,v)), quad frak(m) = (u,v) $
  for $f(u,v) = 3 + 2u + 5v + u^2 + u v$, we have
  $ f mod frak(m)^2 = 3 + 2u + 5v in R/frak(m)^2 $
  where $f(0,0) = 3$, and $d_((0,0)) f = 2u + 5v in T^*_(X,(0,0))$.
]

We can also define the _Zariski tangent space_ at $x$ as the dual of the Zariski cotangent space
$ T_(X,x) := Hom_(kappa(x))(T^*_(X,x), kappa(x)) $
which is a $kappa(x)$ vector space. The dimension of the Zariski tangent space is called the _embedding dimension_ of $X$ at $x$, denoted as $"edim"_x X := dim_(kappa(x)) T_(X,x)$.

#proposition[
  Define $k[epsilon]/(epsilon^2)$ to be the ring of _dual numbers_ over $k$, then we have a natural isomorphism of vector spaces
  $ T_(X,x) simeq Hom_(k,x) (Spec k[epsilon]/(epsilon^2), X) $
  the RHS means $k$-scheme morphisms mapping the only point of $Spec k[epsilon]/(epsilon^2)$ to $x$.
]

#proof[
  You might able to proof this after we completed this lecture.
]

= Deformatinons

== Square-zero Extensions

Now we have the Zariski cotangent space, one thing we might ask is, why we are using $k[epsilon]/(epsilon^2)$ to define the tangent space, can we extend the infinitesimal direction $k epsilon$ to be any module? This gives the concept of _square-zero extensions_. 

#definition(title:[Trivial Square-zero Extension])[
  For a ring $A$ and an $A$-module $M$, we can define the _trivial square-zero extension_ of $A$ by $M$ to be $A plus.o M$, with the multiplication defined as
  $ (a,m) dot (a',m') := (a dot a', a dot m' + a' dot m) $
  for $a,a' in A$ and $m,m' in M$. It is easy to check that this is a ring, and the ideal $M$ satisfies $M^2 = 0$.  
]

#example[
  For a field $k$, the trivial square-zero extension of $k$ by $k$ is exactly the ring of dual numbers $k[epsilon]/(epsilon^2)$.
]

#definition(title:[Square-zero Extension])[
  A _square-zero extension_ is an exact sequence of rings
  $ 0 -> I -> A' -> A -> 0 $
  where $I^2 = 0$ and $I$ is an $A$-module via the map $A' -> A$.
]
